# SAEA-E-004 "Wave-1 substrate ablation ladder" (exploration-plan section 2,
# W-A probes A-1a / A-2a / A-3a).
#
# Six arms over the SAME five earnings21 discovery samples (split
# wave1-e21-sub5-sample, configs/dataset/wave1-e21-sub5.json), all
# obs-agent-loop-ojw so results stay comparable with SAEA-E-002 (R4), all
# evidence_source "none" so the substrate is the only variable.
#
# ONE control run serves all three contrasts. The three probes each need the
# identical bare-core-ojw arm, and because the experiment fragment is part of
# the config hash, keeping them in one experiment is what makes that sharing
# legitimate. It saves two redundant control runs (~2 h) and, more
# importantly, removes control-to-control variance between the contrasts:
# every delta below is measured against literally the same numbers.
#
# Exploratory, n=5, not powered for a precise estimate. Each contrast reports
# a paired exact Wilcoxon whose achievable two-sided p floor at n=5 is
# 2/2**5 = 0.0625 -- no contrast here can reach p<0.05 no matter how clean the
# separation, so read effect sizes and direction, never significance.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contrast 1
# A-1a boundary policy: control (obs_boundary_mode "model") vs fixedwindow
# ("fixed", every other obs_* field unchanged, so the arm always advances by
# the full buffer instead of asking the model for a boundary).
# Hypothesis: the model-guided boundary lands cuts on sentence boundaries
# rather than mid-word and reduces WER by >=1 pp.
# A null result means the three-tier boundary machinery (model estimate -> VAD
# snap -> clamp) is not earning its per-slice prompt and parse cost, and
# fixed-window slicing becomes the cheaper default substrate for every
# downstream OBS/ORG/SUPPLY/USE probe. Inspect the boundary-tier histogram
# next, do not keep tuning this arm.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contrast 2
# A-2a buffer geometry: buf30 vs control (60 s) vs buf90. The 30 s and 90 s
# arms scale min/max/fallback advance PROPORTIONALLY (10/27/25 and 30/82/75
# against the 60 s reference 20/55/50) so the contrast tests buffer size alone
# rather than "buffer size plus a different advance regime"; every scaled
# triple satisfies ObsLoopConfig.from_values's own invariant
# (0 < min <= max <= buffer; min <= fallback <= max).
# Hypothesis: WER trades against slices-per-call, with a visible knee -- a
# short buffer gives less context per decode, a long one risks losing the
# start of a long utterance to prompt pressure.
# A null result (flat curve across 30/60/90 s) means buffer geometry is not a
# lever worth tuning in isolation over this range; treat 60 s as good enough
# and spend the tuning effort on boundary policy or preprocessing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contrast 3
# A-3a preprocessing, two independent single-field changes read against the
# same control:
#   vadtrim-off   obs_vad_trim_min_seconds 0, which genuinely disables the
#                 pre-trim (the loop gates the trim-span computation on > 0)
#                 rather than shrinking it.
#   loudnorm      obs_preprocess ["rms-norm"], per-slice RMS gain applied
#                 after cutting (reproduction/obs_audio.py::rms_normalize).
# Hypotheses: trimming non-speech keeps silence out of the decode budget and
# should not hurt WER; normalising per-slice level helps quiet segments on a
# carrier with speaker-to-speaker level variation.
# Null results mean, respectively, that the trim's case rests on cost
# (trimmed seconds) and not on quality, and that per-slice loudness is not a
# limiting factor for this carrier at this buffer size -- do not add
# normalisation to the default substrate.
#
# run_id:baseline_fragment
SAEA-E-004-control:bare-core-ojw
SAEA-E-004-fixedwindow:a1a-fixed-window-ojw
SAEA-E-004-buf30:a2a-buffer30-ojw
SAEA-E-004-buf90:a2a-buffer90-ojw
SAEA-E-004-vadtrim-off:a3a-vadtrim-off-ojw
SAEA-E-004-loudnorm:a3a-loudnorm-ojw
